message.content:
Below is a minimal example showing how you would ask the **Elasticsearch test‑framework** helper (`SecureMockMaker.createSpy`) to wrap your target object into a spy.  
Just replace the placeholder variable names with whatever concrete instances you’re working with.

> ⚠️ **Important:** In the real test harness the *values* passed to the tool must be serialised Java objects – usually their fully‑qualified class name plus constructor args, e.g. `"org.elasticsearch.test.MockCreationSettings(...)"`. If you’re running inside a JVM test runner you’ll normally hand over the raw objects themselves rather than a stringified form.

---

### Example Invocation (JavaScript/Node)

```js
// Assuming you’re in a Node environment where the ES test helpers expose a JS wrapper.
// Import the helper first (the exact import path depends on your build setup).
import { SecureMockMaker } from '@elastic/elasticsearch-test-framework';

// Your existing objects / configurations
const mockSettings   = /* …your MockCreationSettings… */;
const mockHandler    = /* …your MockHandler implementation… */;
const testObject     = /* …the object you want to spy on… */;

// Create the spy
try {
  const spyInstance = await SecureMockMaker.createSpy(
      mockSettings,          // Settings controlling mock behaviour
      mockHandler,           // Handler defining what happens when methods are invoked
      testObject             // Target object to turn into a spy
  );

  console.log('✅ Spy created:', spyInstance);
} catch (err) {
  console.error('❌ Failed to create spy', err);
}
```

If you prefer to keep everything strictly typed in Java, the equivalent method signature looks roughly like this:

```java
public static <T> T createSpy(MockCreationSettings<T> settings,
                              MockHandler<T> handler,
                              T objectToSpy)
        throws Exception {
    return SecureMockMaker.createSpy(settings, handler, objectToSpy);
}
```

And then you’d call it in your JUnit test:

```java
MyService serviceUnderTest = new MyService();
MockCreationSettings<MyService> settings = ... ;
MockHandler<MyService> handler = ... ;

MyService spy = SecureMockMaker.createSpy(settings, handler, serviceUnderTest);
// Now ‘spy’ behaves exactly like the original object but records calls etc.
```

--- 

#### Quick Checklist

| Step | What to Do |
|------|------------|
| 1️⃣ | Build/populate `mockSettings` – contains flags such as “allow missing mocks”, “default answers” etc. |
| 2️⃣ | Implement/provide `mockHandler` – defines custom logic executed whenever a mocked method is hit. |
| 3️⃣ | Pass the real instance (`testObject`) you wish to observe. |
| 4️⃣ | Invoke `createSpy(...)`; capture the returned proxy/spying instance. |

Once you’ve got the spy, you can assert interactions, verify method invocations, stub behaviours, etc., all while keeping the underlying business logic untouched—exactly what unit tests expect!

